@0    = ~Ah, a new face! You look kinder than those cold guards. Dear <PRO_SIRMAAM>, you wouldn't have anything to warm me up? It is so cold and damp in this place. Just a small thing so I can make a little fire here on the stones...~
@1    = ~Do not listen to that man. He's a pyromaniac who burned down half the lower city. He's in here because he would do it again. He won't get his hands on any flammable substances ever again.~
@2    = ~Just something really small. A toothpick would do! I already burned all my hair...~
@3    = ~Just a tiny little thing I could burn? No one would know!~
@4    = ~See the flames, the flames!~
@5    = ~I miss the light, the bright flames, the heat—and the screams!~
@6    = ~How about you remove my shackles? It would give me the freedom to *move* in a way to show you my gratitude, handsome.~
@7    = ~Who are you?~
@8    = ~What are you suggesting?~
@9    = ~Are you trying to seduce me?~
@10   = ~Nice try. I'm not falling for your tricks.~
@11   = ~Lady, you are in here for a reason. I am pretty sure of that.~
@12   = ~Since you are in here you are not innocent!~
@13   = ~Hmm, now that sounds like the treatment I'd deserve.~
@14   = ~I don't want to talk to you.~
@15   = ~A combination of unfavorable circumstances, nothing more! But you are right, there is a reason for everything. The reason for me showing you my gratitude, for example, would be you releasing me from this prison, hmm?~
@16   = ~Oh, you are definitely right that I am not... *innocent*. Free me, and I will show you just what a naughty girl I can be...~
@17   = ~Well, I think you already know.~
@18   = ~Can you blame me for trying when coincidence blesses me with meeting someone like you?~
@19   = ~Oh, I was hoping we could hold off on the chit chat until after you free me from these heavy chains! I'd be all yours then, for any... examination you'd fancy, handsome.~
@20   = ~[no stats > 17]It must have been fate who brought us together!~
@21   = ~[PC's STR > 17]It must have been fate who brought me together with such a *strong* <PRO_MANWOMAN>!~
@22   = ~[PC's INT > 17]It must have been fate who brought me together with such a *smart* <PRO_MANWOMAN>!~
@23   = ~[PC's WIS > 17]It must have been fate who brought me together with such a *wise* <PRO_MANWOMAN>!~
@24   = ~[PC's DEX > 17]It must have been fate who brought me together with such a *skilled* <PRO_MANWOMAN>!~
@25   = ~[PC's CON > 17]It must have been fate who brought me together with such a *tough* <PRO_MANWOMAN>!~
@26   = ~[PC's CHR > 17]It must have been fate who brought me together with such an *impressive* <PRO_MANWOMAN>!~
@27   = ~[Safana]*scoffs* <CHARNAME>, please tell me you see that wench for what she is—a manipulator who's trying to wiggle her way out of *prison* by buttering her words up seductively. I know *exactly* where this'll end. She'll laugh in your face the moment you open those shackles.~
@28   = ~Oh, so you're projecting, hmm? I don't know what your history with that woman is, dear stranger, but don't blame me for instantly falling for someone so handsome. I'd say I'm not that bad myself, hmm?~
@29   = ~[Safana]Phhhh.~
@30   = ~Hmm, I do think you're not bad, indeed.~
@31   = ~I am sorry. Even if I wanted to, I can't let you just go.~
@32   = ~You are so not my type.~
@33   = ~Don't bother any further. You'll stay exactly where you are.~
@34   = ~Don't blame me for instantly falling for someone so handsome. I'd say I'm not that bad myself, hmm?~
@35   = ~Oh, I understand! But will you at least open these shackles? I'd like to show you my gratitude, but with these chains, I can barely move.~
@36   = ~So it is settled, then?~
@37   = ~Oh, so <PRO_HESHE> is not only handsome, but also honest and direct! You can't leave me here like this. Not after meeting you the way we did!~
@38   = ~I'm sure such a heroic person as you won't have a problem with opening the locks that hold these chains.~
@39   = ~Tell me who you are, first.~
@40   = ~I'm not freeing a criminal.~
@41   = ~Oh, they say I'm an Amnian spy! Can you believe that? They put me into this prison and chained me to this wall! Even if I were from Amn—can you blame the Amnians for wanting to know what is happening? Everyone was talking about war! Can you imagine a *war* between the Sword Coast and Amn?~
@42   = ~You are right, this shouldn't be a reason to chain you up like this.~
@43   = ~So... Can I hope that there won't be any chains on me when I show you my gratitude for freeing me?~
@44   = ~Hmm, now that does sound good. Here—your shackles are gone. Now come here...~
@45   = ~You do not need to do any such thing. I removed your chains nonetheless. You are free to go.~
@46   = ~Absolutely not. You stay right where you are. I don't believe a word you are saying.~
@47   = ~Were you a mere spy, I don't think they'd chain you up like that. No, there is more you didn't tell me. You will stay right here.~
@48   = ~What—you really thought "come here" would be part of what we'll do now? Ahahaha!~
@49   = ~Free from the chains at last! Thank you so much, my *savior*! (laughs) Thanks for setting me free, I thought I'd have to do it myself.~
@50   = ~Aww. Seems you are not only handsome but also have brains. Very well then. Can't blame a girl for trying.~
@51   = ~Oh, don't hurt me, please! I'm such an innocent woman. (laughs)~
@52   = ~Hmm, what a pity. Come back soon, handsome!~
@53   = ~Aww, look at who it is.~
@54   = ~My savior. (laughs)~
@55   = ~The <PRO_MANWOMAN> who broke my heart! See, I made my own way here. (laughs)~
@56   = ~What are you doing here?~
@57   = ~So you *are* an Amnian spy, investigating the Iron Throne?~
@58   = ~I should punish you where you stand, for being the escaped criminal that you are!~
@59   = ~I haven't forgotten how you tricked me, you know.~
@60   = ~Let's just pretend we didn't see each other.~
@61   = ~Hmm. All right, I'll take the deal—but only because you asked so nicely. Take care, handsome.~
@62   = ~Oh, doesn't it fit together in your small head? "But if she's from Amn, shouldn't she be on *my* side in this conflict?"~
@63   = ~I am not working for the Iron Throne, and yet, here I am, trying to *escalate* the conflict between Amn and Baldur's Gate. Complicated, isn't it?~
@64   = ~So we are enemies, I'm afraid.~
@65   = ~I don't want to fight you. Can we just pretend we didn't see each other?~
@66   = ~Aww, you are getting cuter every time we meet.~
@67   = ~My presence here is simple, really—forces inside the Iron Throne are working for war on the Sword Coast, and I am, too, for my own masters.~
@68   = ~What? Who are you working for, then?~
@69   = ~...That makes us enemies?~
@70   = ~We are, handsome—you are endangering my mission, so I'll kill you here and now.~
@71   = ~Oh, now look at who we have here. <CHARNAME>, the bandit camp crasher. Well, I made it out of there alive, but turns out the Flaming Fist pansies are no fools, either.~
@72   = ~You're a bandit? Seems I missed one scalp.~
@73   = ~You're not a normal bandit if they put you here.~
@74   = ~Ain't I the lucky one, then?~
@75   = ~What about I take yours now?~
@76   = ~Hmm, you could say that, but I wasn't a division leader, either. I was just... a little bit better informed than those fools. Didn't help me much, though, except to "stay alive".~
@77   = ~Better informed? What do you know then?~
@78   = ~How about I change the latter for you?~
@79   = ~Talking to you is pointless.~
@80   = ~What—here, now? Aren't you afraid of the guards watching while you go around slaying their prisoners?~
@81   = ~No, I'm not.~
@82   = ~Well, what choice did I have with you attacking me first—you understand?~
@83   = ~I was just shaking my fist. Although your presence in here means you were more than a minion.~
@84   = ~I was just shaking my fist. What do you know then, if you were so well informed?~
@85   = ~Well, everyone thought we are being hired by the Zhents. *I* knew it wasn't them.~
@86   = ~Who was it then?~
@87   = ~What, you think I'd just tell you?~
@88   = ~I guess it doesn't matter. It's not like I think you could tell me much.~
@89   = ~You need some "motivation", huh?~
@90   = ~All right, all right! It's the Iron Throne. *They* stirred up all the trouble, probably to profit from the chaos. There, I told you!~
@91   = ~You've got to be kidding me! Man, it's a shame we're on different sides.~
@92   = ~Well, I'm afraid *you*'ll have to leave. I'm kind of stuck here.~
@93   = ~Listen, I know I was on the opposite side. But truth be told—wouldn't you agree I'd be of more use outside this prison? I never went after you and didn't care about some personal agenda of the higher-ups. There are no more bandit activities, I won't cause you any problems.~
@94   = ~Someone is recruiting mercenaries up at the High Moor, that's where we'll be going. I'll be out of your hair and no harm done to this city, either, see?~
@95   = ~All you'd have to do is bring this note to a contact of mine. Will you do that?~
@96   = ~Pff, you'd reveal more bandits to me? Do you wish death to your "contact"?~
@97   = ~What would be in it for me?~
@98   = ~No, I won't.~
@99   = ~Conspiring, eh? Guess I do have to end you after all.~
@100  = ~I was afraid you'd say that.~
@101  = ~I'm not that stupid, don't worry.~
@102  = ~A nice sum of gold.~
@103  = ~I'll tell you where to put the note, and how to find the hidden gold. You just put the note in a specific place—that's it. I trust you, you won't go running to the Flaming Fist about this. And even if—there is nothing to tell them, other than that you accepted a note you won't be able to read and that they'll need to guard a place where no one will show.~
@104  = ~Fine, I'll do it. That reward better be worth it.~
@105  = ~It will. Here is the note. If you leave the city by the bridge and head north, there will be one single tree that usually has red leaves around this time of year. You need to loosen the bark a little at the lowest branch hole. Just hide the note in there. You'll find gold in there as well. The safety stash is already there, and I wrote them they should put some more in when they take it out. Thanks, man.~
@106  = ~I'm counting on you.~
@107  = ~Did you do it?~
@108  = ~You still here?~
@109  = ~Talona, I sacrificed them all for you! All for you. Take me to you, my goddess, Mother of All Plagues! I did it for you—they all drank the blessed waters, they all fell ill, all died in your name! The streets—full of corpses, to give glory to you! Why... why have you forsaken me...~
@110  = ~Take me to you, my goddess, Mother of All Plagues!~
@111  = ~All for you, only for you!~
@112  = ~Why have you forsaken me?~
@113  = ~It finally happened! The guards have been bribed at last, and you're here to avenge your dead relatives! Guards! GUARDS!—Ah, it's no use calling them if they let you through! My fate is sealed!~
@114  = ~Calm yourself, I'm not here for any such thing.~
@115  = ~I thought your fate was already sealed, with you being here in this prison and all.~
@116  = ~Oh, so you are a murderer? Maybe I *should* avenge dead people then!~
@117  = ~*yawn* So, you are a murderer. Why am I not surprised? Oh, wait—maybe because this is the Seatower Prison.~
@118  = ~I shouldn't be talking to you.~
@119  = ~Who—who are you then?~
@120  = ~I am <CHARNAME>. Who are you?~
@121  = ~Tell me why you are here.~
@122  = ~Noooo!...~
@123  = ~So you have come to mock me. Isn't being here enough punishment?~
@124  = ~I just wanted to talk to you, is all.~
@125  = ~I don't know, what was it you did?~
@126  = ~I did nothing!~
@127  = ~Oh, come *on*! You wouldn't be here then.~
@128  = ~You just mentioned dead people. Maybe you should stop denying your crimes!~
@129  = ~"Nothing", huh? And what exactly was that "nothing" that brought you here?~
@130  = ~My name is Dorsay. I was a sea captain once...~
@131  = ~It—it is true! I did nothing... when the first sailors became sick, I did nothing... when half the crew lay down and the first died, when the symptoms were clearly visible... I did nothing, because I wanted to have the goods unloaded. I denied that there was a threat to the city when we go ashore. There are rules for quarantine—I quarantined the crew, but not the wares. How should I know that it was really the plague we brought from Chult! Ah, if you are not here to hurt me and aren't guards either, best you leave me to my fate.~
@132  = ~The sea... she's calling to me. So near and yet so far away...~
@133  = ~Then you better go!~
@134  = ~Who... who are you? You are no guards.~
@135  = ~I am <CHARNAME>, and I gained access to this prison and am allowed to talk to the prisoners.~
@136  = ~I'm posing the questions. Tell me who you are.~
@137  = ~You look surprisingly sophisticated for someone who committed crimes that led to life imprisonment in this dungeon.~
@138  = ~Ah, another murderer waiting for their justice!~
@139  = ~Such... such a friendly face. It's been years since someone addressed me so kindly.~
@140  = ~Why are you here?~
@141  = ~Well, that could be over soon, depending on why you are here. Tell me!~
@142  = ~I'm afraid that was my good deed for the day. Now you'll have to answer for the murder you committed!~
@143  = ~No, please! No! I-I did not kill anyone. Don't you even know what I'm being accused of?~
@144  = ~Please tell me.~
@145  = ~And what would that be?~
@146  = ~No, and I don't want to hear it, either.~
@147  = ~Oh, this is the dungeon for the real dangerous criminals, not just thugs and murderers. I'm not saying that I *am* such a criminal...~
@148  = ~*sigh* Of course not. Let me guess—you are innocent?~
@149  = ~If you are in here, I am sure you are at least a murderer!~
@150  = ~I am Timothy Malastair. I am cousin to Duke Eltan. I was accused... I was convicted of orchestrating an assassination attempt on Duke Eltan, which is high treason. Had I intended to increase my family shares of the Flaming Fist, this would at least have made sense, but I was aspirant to a leadership role in the Iron Throne at that time! Yes, I know it is lame that I insist I am innocent, but it is the truth! I thank you for listening to me. I don't expect you to believe me, but it felt good to be able to talk about it, at least.~
@151  = ~And I can't hold it against you.~
@152  = ~As much as I enjoyed talking to you, I know you have more pressing matters to attend to.~
@153  = ~I have here evidence for your innocence!~
@154  = ~I'll be going.~
@155  = ~You... how... After all these years?! This is brilliant! Please, hand it over to Eltan or some other authority quickly!~
@156  = ~(truth) I will.~
@157  = ~You see no problem in Duke Eltan getting this? The accusation was that you tried to kill him, after all. He might hold a grudge or feel embarrassed that he was wrong.~
@158  = ~Yes, you see, I was thinking—this must be very valuable to you, don't you think?~
@159  = ~What? No, I wasn't planning on helping you. I wanted to show this to you and laugh into your face!~
@160  = ~Oh, thank you—thank you! Ah, my heart—I need to calm myself a little, it feels like I'm going to burst. To dream of freedom, after all these years...~
@161  = ~What? Oh, no. Eltan is not like that. He takes these matters very seriously—which means that he put me here when the evidence pointed to my guilt, but he'll make sure I'll be freed if that is no longer the case. Please, give it to him, or the Seatower Commander, who might be more available.~
@162  = ~Is it—is it gold that you want? I can send a letter to my mother and have someone meet you. They will be there! How does... 3,000 gold sound to you?~
@163  = ~That sounds great! Where will I meet her?~
@164  = ~Ah, that look of hope in your eyes! It's priceless. And now we'll let it die. See this note with the evidence? Woops, how clumsy of me—now it's nothing but shreds. I don't think anyone can still read what was written on it.~
@165  = ~Let's say... behind the Temple of Helm, in the north of Baldur's Gate. Please, go there and hand over the note. You'll be my savior, regardless of the gold you are requesting.~
@166  = ~No! No—how can—how can you—the evidence! After all this time... so near... I...~
@167  = ~Ah, it is you! <CHARNAME>, the city's savior—and my very personal one, too! The Dukes have asked me to oversee what will happen to this building. Who would have thought that the years in the dungeons would now be the reason why I enjoy the full trust of the dukes and, so to speak, have great influence on the reconstruction of the local branch. The ways of the gods are indeed inscrutable.~
@168  = ~By the way, my mother wants you to have this, as a small compensation for your troubles. She explicitly apologizes for not showing her gratitude in a more appropriate manner.~
@169  = ~Well, I need to leave and ponder how this can be salvaged and how much I want to be involved. It was nice to see you again, <CHARNAME>. Farewell.~
@170  = ~<CHARNAME>! Greetings. Look what this place turned into. It's not exactly how I pictured this to go. But, a merchant must seize opportunities when they arise.~
@171  = ~The Dukes have asked me to oversee what will happen to this building. Who would have thought that the years in the dungeons would now be the reason why I enjoy the full trust of the dukes and, so to speak, have great influence on the reconstruction of the local branch. The ways of the gods are indeed inscrutable.~
@172  = ~I am glad to see you well. I thank you for the gold.~
@173  = ~I am glad to see you well.~
@174  = ~I am in your debt, <CHARNAME>.~
@175  = ~Opportunities? You are not charging these poor people for staying here, are you?~
@176  = ~So you are making gold out of the building being turned into a nursery. You are a real salesperson, I have to say.~
@177  = ~Great to see you are busy, but I need to go.~
@178  = ~What? No, absolutely not!—But we do sell the bandages to the priests and get a compensation from the dukes.~
@179  = ~Well, it all costs and the building *does* suffer. It will need some reconstruction when these troubled times are over.~
@180  = ~I won't keep you any longer. I am sure we all have lots to do. Farewell, my savior.~
@181  = ~Good evening. Are you here on account of <CHARNAME>?~
@182  = ~Yes, <PRO_HESHE> sent me.~
@183  = ~No, <PRO_HESHE> will be here shortly.~
@184  = ~I am waiting for the evidence for my son's innocence. Please hasten and bring it to me.~
@185  = ~So you came. Show me the evidence, please.~
@186  = ~Here, take it for free. I decided not to make gold out of your family's misery.~
@187  = ~Here you are. Give me the agreed sum.~
@188  = ~I will, but the price just went up. It's 5,000 gold now.~
@189  = ~What, no words of thanks, of hope that your son will go free again, no tears, no emotions?~
@190  = ~Ah, I changed my mind. I'll go now.~
@191  = ~Please, if it is more gold you want—we will pay more.~
@192  = ~All right. 5,000 gold and this is yours.~
@193  = ~Nah, I just changed my mind. I will hold onto it—maybe light a fire with it, or use it when I go behind the bushes... who knows.~
@194  = ~Oh no... How can someone be so cruel? My guards are not strong enough to take you on, and we both know it. Change your mind and come back, I beg you.~
@195  = ~I would be... very thankful if you would hand over the evidence that will free my son from his unmerited prison sentence... my <PRO_LADYLORD>.~
@196  = ~See? That wasn't so hard. Here you are.~
@197  = ~I thank you. There seems to be a last spark of honor in you.~
@198  = ~I thank you. There seems to be some sort of honor in you left, if one can call it that.~
@199  = ~I did not expect otherwise, but still, I thank you.~
@200  = ~I will be leaving now. You'd be wise not to stir any trouble.~
@201  = ~This is close enough.~
@202  = ~Take a step back, please.~
@203  = ~Talk to Her Ladyship, not me.~
@204  = ~I have evidence here to prove that your cousin Malastair is innocent of the attempt on your life for which he was convicted. It was Sarevok all along!~
@205  = ~Timothy? He would be innocent? Let me have this at once!~
@206  = ~Ah—just how far do this snake's intrigues go?! I thank you for bringing this to me. I swear, as soon as the situation is resolved and I have full command of the Flaming Fist again, I will see to it that Timothy gets to his rights. I pray to the gods that you will succeed in stopping Sarevok, <CHARNAME>.~
@207  = ~Ah—just how far did this snake's intrigues go?! I thank you for bringing this to my attention. I swear on my honor as commander of the Flaming Fist that I will see to it that Timothy gets to his rights.~
@208  = ~<CHARNAME>, I also heard it was you who found the evidence that my cousin, Timothy Malastair—convicted of an assassination attempt on me—was indeed innocent. I thank you for handing it in and giving our family a chance to heal. Our gratitude is yours.~
@209  = ~Timothy? He would be innocent? Let me have this at once! Ah—just how far did this snake's intrigues go?! I thank you for bringing this to my attention. I swear on my honor as commander of the Flaming Fist that I will see to it that Timothy gets to his rights.~
